---
title: Motion
description: Optional SVG tween and spring motion, definition-local timing, interruption behavior, reduced motion, and the standalone spring sampler.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Motion is an optional renderer. Core definitions, scene compilation, static SVG,\nand Canvas do not import a clock or physics solver."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"motion","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { motion } from '@tanstack/charts/motion'\n\nconst renderer = motion({\n  transition: { type: 'spring', stiffness: 170, damping: 18, mass: 1 },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"function motion(options?: ChartMotionOptions): UniversalChartRenderer\n\nfunction motion<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n>(options?: ChartMotionOptions): ChartRenderer<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n\ninterface ChartMotionOptions {\n  initial?: boolean | 'always'\n  transition?: ChartMotionTransition\n  respectReducedMotion?: boolean\n  resize?: boolean\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion(options)"},{"type":"text","value":" beside a typed definition and let the host infer its\ndatum and axis values. The explicit generic overload remains available when a\nlow-level caller must type the renderer before it has a definition."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initial"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Animate first client paint; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"always"},{"type":"text","value":" also replays adopted SVG"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"transition"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1,100 ms tween with the default entrance ease"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Renderer-wide fallback"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"respectReducedMotion"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Snap when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"prefers-reduced-motion: reduce"},{"type":"text","value":" matches"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resize"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Animate updates caused only by a chart size change"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server-rendered SVG is adopted without replaying entrance motion by default.\nSet "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initial: 'always'"},{"type":"text","value":" when a hydrated chart should replay the same entrance\nas a client-only mount. Keyed updates start from painted geometry. An\ninterrupted spring carries its sampled value and velocity into the new target.\nA spring has no duration; it finishes when both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"restSpeed"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"restDelta"},{"type":"text","value":" are\nsatisfied, with a 10-second safety limit."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Initial choreography follows geometry: Cartesian bars and paths grow from\ntheir semantic baseline, radial lines and areas grow from the polar center,\nand arcs sweep through their authored angle. Keyed removals stay painted\nthrough their exit transition."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data-less "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"crosshair"},{"type":"text","value":" marks use the same keyed focus-motion path. Rapid pointer\nor keyboard retargeting preserves the guide elements and incoming spring\nvelocity; labels remain aligned to their moving rules."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inline mark "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"states"},{"type":"text","value":" wait while entrance or data motion is active. The latest\nstate is applied when that motion settles. Applying another geometry\ntransition to the same marks would interrupt the running data transition.\nThis includes radius changes such as growing a focused dot. A continuously\nupdating chart can keep these states deferred."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a separate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"whenFocused"},{"type":"text","value":" mark for a focus indicator that must appear\nduring entrance motion. The focus layer has its own visibility and does not\nreplace the animated mark's geometry:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { dot, lineY, whenFocused } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst marks = [\n  lineY(rows, { x: 'x', y: 'y' }),\n  dot(rows, { x: 'x', y: 'y', r: 2 }),\n  whenFocused(dot(rows, { id: 'focus-dots', x: 'x', y: 'y', r: 6 }), {\n    match: 'x',\n  }),\n]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The built-in HTML tooltip also consumes this renderer's transition. Entry,\nmovement, retargeting, and exit therefore use the same spring without copying\nthe transition into the chart definition. A static renderer keeps the tooltip\nimmediate and does not import the motion runtime."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the renderer-neutral host in vanilla applications:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { mountChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/renderer'\nimport { motion } from '@tanstack/charts/motion'\n\nconst host = mountChartRenderer(container, {\n  definition,\n  renderer: motion(),\n  width: 640,\n  height: 360,\n  ariaLabel: 'Monthly revenue',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React and Octane applications use their "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/core"},{"type":"text","value":" component entry and pass the\nsame renderer. Other adapters start with their default SVG renderer, and a\ndefinition may still select Canvas for individual marks."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"definition-local-motion","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Definition-local motion"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion"},{"type":"text","value":" on a definition, mark, axis, tick collection, tick-label collection,\nor axis label is inert policy. The optional renderer consumes it. Definitions\nremain valid for static SVG and Canvas, which paint the final state."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This stays true in a mixed chart. A mark that selects "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"canvasChartRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":"\nkeeps its authored motion option, but the Canvas layer paints the final scene\ninstead of running the tween or spring policy. The optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion()"},{"type":"text","value":" renderer\nstill animates the default-renderer layers it owns."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  motion: {\n    transition: { type: 'spring', stiffness: 170, damping: 18 },\n  },\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, {\n      id: 'forecast',\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'forecast',\n      key: 'id',\n      motion: { transition: { type: 'spring', mass: 1.25 } },\n    }),\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'actual',\n      key: 'id',\n      motion(context) {\n        return {\n          delay: context.phase === 'enter' ? context.datumIndex * 35 : 0,\n        }\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleBand,\n      axis: {\n        ticks: { motion: { transition: { type: 'tween', duration: 180 } } },\n        tickLabels: { motion: { delay: 40 } },\n      },\n    },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The cascade is renderer default, chart, mark, axis, specific guide, then the\nactive focus-state transition. Same-type transitions inherit omitted fields.\nSet "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion: false"},{"type":"text","value":" at any definition scope to suppress inherited motion for\nthat scope. A more specific child can re-enable motion with its own definition.\nAn authored "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delay"},{"type":"text","value":" replaces automatic entrance staggering for that target.\nSpring updates begin immediately even when a definition returns a delay, so a\nretarget cannot freeze incoming momentum. Spring enter and exit delays, and\ntween delays in every phase, are honored."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All built-in marks accept "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMarkMotionOptions<TDatum>"},{"type":"text","value":". Nested polar marks\nalso accept "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion"},{"type":"text","value":"; their timing is merged below the parent "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"polar"},{"type":"text","value":" mark."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scope"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Motion input"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Renderer fallback"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion({ transition })"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whole chart"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineChart({ motion })"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Any built-in mark"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The mark's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion"},{"type":"text","value":" option"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Axis, including its grid lines"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x.axis.motion"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y.axis.motion"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick rules"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.ticks.motion"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick labels"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.tickLabels.motion"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Axis label"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.label.motion"},{"type":"text","value":" when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"label"},{"type":"text","value":" is an object"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Crosshair or focus guide"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The guide mark's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion"},{"type":"text","value":" option"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"HTML tooltip"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inherits chart motion; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tooltip.motion"},{"type":"text","value":" overrides it"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grid lines use their axis policy because they are part of that axis's guide\nsystem. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tooltip.motion: false"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps the tooltip immediate even when chart\ngeometry animates. Legends and application-owned controls are not marks and do\nnot currently participate in the motion cascade."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"spreading-timing-utilities","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Spreading timing utilities"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the isolated timing entry when the definition only needs motion policy:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { stagger } from '@tanstack/charts/motion/definition'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  motion: {\n    path: 'morph',\n    ...stagger({ each: 35, by: 'series', roles: ['arc', 'bar'] }),\n  },\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stagger()"},{"type":"text","value":" returns one context-aware "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delay"},{"type":"text","value":" field for direct object spread.\nIt uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"datumIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" by default, can use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"seriesIndex"},{"type":"text","value":", defaults to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enter"},{"type":"text","value":"\nphase, and can filter by phase and semantic role. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"offset"},{"type":"text","value":" delays the first\ntarget. Normal object-spread order controls precedence, so an explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delay"},{"type":"text","value":"\nwritten after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"...stagger()"},{"type":"text","value":" replaces it."}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ChartMotionStaggerOptions {\n  each: number\n  offset?: number\n  by?: 'datum' | 'series'\n  phase?: ChartMotionPhase | readonly ChartMotionPhase[]\n  roles?: ChartMotionRole | readonly ChartMotionRole[]\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stagger()"},{"type":"text","value":" is also exported from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/motion"},{"type":"text","value":". The dedicated\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/motion/definition"},{"type":"text","value":" entry excludes the SVG renderer and spring solver."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"timing-types","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Timing types"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type ChartMotionPhase = 'enter' | 'update' | 'exit'\n\ninterface ChartMotionTweenTransition {\n  type: 'tween'\n  duration?: number\n  easing?:\n    | 'linear'\n    | 'ease'\n    | 'ease-in'\n    | 'ease-out'\n    | 'ease-in-out'\n    | ((progress: number) => number)\n}\n\ninterface ChartMotionSpringTransition extends ChartSpringOptions {\n  type: 'spring'\n}\n\ntype ChartMotionTransition =\n  ChartMotionTweenTransition | ChartMotionSpringTransition\n\ninterface ChartRollingPathMotion {\n  update: 'rolling'\n  x: 'shift'\n  y?: 'fixed' | 'reproject'\n  fallback?: 'snap' | 'morph'\n}\n\ntype ChartMotionPath = 'morph' | ChartRollingPathMotion\n\ninterface ChartMotionTiming<TDatum = unknown> {\n  delay?: number | ((context: ChartMotionContext<TDatum>) => number | undefined)\n  transition?: ChartMotionTransition\n  path?: ChartMotionPath\n}\n\ntype ChartMotionDefinition<TDatum = unknown> =\n  | false\n  | ChartMotionTiming<TDatum>\n  | ((\n      context: ChartMotionContext<TDatum>,\n    ) => false | ChartMotionTiming<TDatum> | undefined)\n\ninterface ChartMarkMotionOptions<TDatum = unknown> {\n  motion?: ChartMotionDefinition<TDatum>\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMotionContext"},{"type":"text","value":" provides "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"phase"},{"type":"text","value":", semantic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"role"},{"type":"text","value":", stable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":", optional\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"markId"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"seriesKey"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"seriesIndex"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"datumIndex"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"datumCount"},{"type":"text","value":", optional typed\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"datum"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"point"},{"type":"text","value":", and optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMotionRole"},{"type":"text","value":" covers marks, axes,\ngrid lines, ticks, tick labels, and axis labels."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus styles use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMarkStateTransition"},{"type":"text","value":", which is a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMotionTransition"},{"type":"text","value":" plus optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"respectReducedMotion"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"path: 'morph'"},{"type":"text","value":" is the ordinary command-by-command path interpolation. A\nrolling update is configured only with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartRollingPathMotion"},{"type":"text","value":" object."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"rolling-paths","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rolling paths"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Path morphing matches SVG path commands. That is useful when values change in\nplace, but a rolling time series should keep each retained sample intact and\nmove the trace left:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const definition = defineChart({\n  motion: {\n    path: {\n      update: 'rolling',\n      x: 'shift',\n      y: 'reproject',\n      fallback: 'snap',\n    },\n    transition: { type: 'tween', duration: 800, easing: 'linear' },\n  },\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'time', y: 'value', key: 'id' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc().domain([visibleStart, visibleEnd]) },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 100]) },\n  },\n\n  clip: true,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Include enough keyed samples before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"visibleStart"},{"type":"text","value":" to cover the largest\nexpected update batch. A valid rolling update installs the completed path\nbeyond the right clip, applies one affine transform that reproduces the prior\nframe, and animates that transform to identity. Retained samples therefore move\nas one piece while new samples enter from the right."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The renderer validates the update before animating it:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"retained keys must be the exact old suffix and new prefix;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the keyed primitive must remain a line or remain an area;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"added and removed sample counts must balance;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"retained semantic x, y, interval, and group values must be unchanged;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"every retained x coordinate must share one translation;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the plot bounds must be stable and the path must be inside a clip;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"neither the previous nor target path may have a nonzero transient x or y\nviewport translation;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the target geometry must cover both clip edges throughout the shift;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"custom path strings and neighbor-sensitive curves do not use the affine\npath."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Omitting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":" is equivalent to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y: 'fixed'"},{"type":"text","value":" and requires unchanged screen y\ncoordinates. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y: 'reproject'"},{"type":"text","value":" also\naccepts a changed continuous y-domain when one affine y transform maps the new\nprojection back to the previous frame. This preserves the path geometry while\nthe line shifts and the y-axis updates. Keep an area's baseline semantically\nstable, such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y1: 0"},{"type":"text","value":", so both edges can be reprojected."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fallback"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'snap'"},{"type":"text","value":". An update that does not satisfy the rolling\ncontract is installed without an unrelated path morph. Use\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fallback: 'morph'"},{"type":"text","value":" only when command interpolation is the intended failure\nbehavior."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A valid update that interrupts another rolling update composes the transform\ncurrently painted on the path with the next shift, then continues toward the\nnew target. It does not reset to either completed scene. Presentation points,\nfocus marks, and an active tooltip follow the same composed geometry."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authored point dots and default focus circles use the path's rolling timing.\nEntering points begin under the composed transform, exiting points travel out\nthrough the clip, and snap fallback removes stale exiting presentation points\nin the same commit as the path. A focus layer can update immediately during\ndata motion; geometry or style from an inline mark state waits until the active\ndata transition settles so it cannot cancel the rolling transform. Back-to-back\nupdates retain the latest requested state and apply it when the feed becomes\nidle; a feed that never becomes idle keeps state geometry deferred while focus\nlayers and tooltips continue to follow presentation points."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rolling motion rejects a nonzero "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"viewport.translate"},{"type":"text","value":" on either axis because\nthe viewport and path would otherwise own competing transient transforms.\nCommit the viewport domain and reset its translation before the rolling data\nupdate, or expect the configured snap-or-morph fallback."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use fixed plot margins so changing tick-label widths cannot change the affine\nframe. Use linear segments or a curve whose visible control points do not\nchange when a sample is appended."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion()"},{"type":"text","value":" renders SVG clip paths and gradients itself, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clip: true"},{"type":"text","value":" applies\nto the animated marks without a separate resource renderer."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"standalone-spring","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Standalone spring"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createChartSpring } from '@tanstack/charts/spring'\n\nconst spring = createChartSpring({ stiffness: 170, damping: 18, mass: 1 })\nconst sample = spring.sample(16, { from: 0, to: 100, velocity: 0 })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"interface ChartSpringOptions {\n  stiffness?: number\n  damping?: number\n  mass?: number\n  restSpeed?: number\n  restDelta?: number\n}\n\ninterface ChartSpringState {\n  from: number\n  to: number\n  velocity?: number\n}\n\ninterface ChartSpringSample {\n  value: number\n  velocity: number\n  done: boolean\n}\n\ninterface ChartSpring {\n  readonly options: Readonly<Required<ChartSpringOptions>>\n  sample(elapsedMs: number, state?: ChartSpringState): ChartSpringSample\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChartSpring"},{"type":"text","value":" returns an analytic, frame-rate-independent damped harmonic\noscillator. Values and velocities use caller units per second. Seed a new\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartSpringState"},{"type":"text","value":" from the prior sample to preserve momentum across targets."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"compatibility","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compatibility"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compatible numeric attributes and path command skeletons interpolate."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Incompatible element types or path topology use keyed enter/exit opacity."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable keys preserve DOM identity, velocity, and presentation points."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Crosshair rules, bands, labels, and markers follow the same keyed interruption\nbehavior as focused marks."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SVG interaction follows animated presentation geometry for keyed built-in and\ncustom marks."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Static SVG and Canvas ignore definition motion and paint final geometry."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The optional renderer is browser SVG only; it is not a Web Animations, View\nTransitions, Canvas, or native adapter."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"motion","text":"motion","level":2},{"id":"definition-local-motion","text":"Definition-local motion","level":2},{"id":"spreading-timing-utilities","text":"Spreading timing utilities","level":2},{"id":"timing-types","text":"Timing types","level":2},{"id":"rolling-paths","text":"Rolling paths","level":2},{"id":"standalone-spring","text":"Standalone spring","level":2},{"id":"compatibility","text":"Compatibility","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nMotion is an optional renderer. Core definitions, scene compilation, static SVG,\nand Canvas do not import a clock or physics solver.\n\n## `motion`\n\n```ts\nimport { motion } from '@tanstack/charts/motion'\n\nconst renderer = motion({\n  transition: { type: 'spring', stiffness: 170, damping: 18, mass: 1 },\n})\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction motion(options?: ChartMotionOptions): UniversalChartRenderer\n\nfunction motion<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n>(options?: ChartMotionOptions): ChartRenderer<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n\ninterface ChartMotionOptions {\n  initial?: boolean | 'always'\n  transition?: ChartMotionTransition\n  respectReducedMotion?: boolean\n  resize?: boolean\n}\n```\n\nUse `motion(options)` beside a typed definition and let the host infer its\ndatum and axis values. The explicit generic overload remains available when a\nlow-level caller must type the renderer before it has a definition.\n\n| Option                 | Default                                       | Meaning                                                       |\n| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `initial`              | `true`                                        | Animate first client paint; `always` also replays adopted SVG |\n| `transition`           | 1,100 ms tween with the default entrance ease | Renderer-wide fallback                                        |\n| `respectReducedMotion` | `true`                                        | Snap when `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` matches            |\n| `resize`               | `false`                                       | Animate updates caused only by a chart size change            |\n\nServer-rendered SVG is adopted without replaying entrance motion by default.\nSet `initial: 'always'` when a hydrated chart should replay the same entrance\nas a client-only mount. Keyed updates start from painted geometry. An\ninterrupted spring carries its sampled value and velocity into the new target.\nA spring has no duration; it finishes when both `restSpeed` and `restDelta` are\nsatisfied, with a 10-second safety limit.\n\nInitial choreography follows geometry: Cartesian bars and paths grow from\ntheir semantic baseline, radial lines and areas grow from the polar center,\nand arcs sweep through their authored angle. Keyed removals stay painted\nthrough their exit transition.\n\nData-less `crosshair` marks use the same keyed focus-motion path. Rapid pointer\nor keyboard retargeting preserves the guide elements and incoming spring\nvelocity; labels remain aligned to their moving rules.\n\nInline mark `states` wait while entrance or data motion is active. The latest\nstate is applied when that motion settles. Applying another geometry\ntransition to the same marks would interrupt the running data transition.\nThis includes radius changes such as growing a focused dot. A continuously\nupdating chart can keep these states deferred.\n\nUse a separate `whenFocused` mark for a focus indicator that must appear\nduring entrance motion. The focus layer has its own visibility and does not\nreplace the animated mark's geometry:\n\n```ts\nimport { dot, lineY, whenFocused } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst marks = [\n  lineY(rows, { x: 'x', y: 'y' }),\n  dot(rows, { x: 'x', y: 'y', r: 2 }),\n  whenFocused(dot(rows, { id: 'focus-dots', x: 'x', y: 'y', r: 6 }), {\n    match: 'x',\n  }),\n]\n```\n\nThe built-in HTML tooltip also consumes this renderer's transition. Entry,\nmovement, retargeting, and exit therefore use the same spring without copying\nthe transition into the chart definition. A static renderer keeps the tooltip\nimmediate and does not import the motion runtime.\n\nUse the renderer-neutral host in vanilla applications:\n\n```ts\nimport { mountChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/renderer'\nimport { motion } from '@tanstack/charts/motion'\n\nconst host = mountChartRenderer(container, {\n  definition,\n  renderer: motion(),\n  width: 640,\n  height: 360,\n  ariaLabel: 'Monthly revenue',\n})\n```\n\nReact and Octane applications use their `/core` component entry and pass the\nsame renderer. Other adapters start with their default SVG renderer, and a\ndefinition may still select Canvas for individual marks.\n\n## Definition-local motion\n\n`motion` on a definition, mark, axis, tick collection, tick-label collection,\nor axis label is inert policy. The optional renderer consumes it. Definitions\nremain valid for static SVG and Canvas, which paint the final state.\n\nThis stays true in a mixed chart. A mark that selects `canvasChartRenderer`\nkeeps its authored motion option, but the Canvas layer paints the final scene\ninstead of running the tween or spring policy. The optional `motion()` renderer\nstill animates the default-renderer layers it owns.\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  motion: {\n    transition: { type: 'spring', stiffness: 170, damping: 18 },\n  },\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, {\n      id: 'forecast',\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'forecast',\n      key: 'id',\n      motion: { transition: { type: 'spring', mass: 1.25 } },\n    }),\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'actual',\n      key: 'id',\n      motion(context) {\n        return {\n          delay: context.phase === 'enter' ? context.datumIndex * 35 : 0,\n        }\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleBand,\n      axis: {\n        ticks: { motion: { transition: { type: 'tween', duration: 180 } } },\n        tickLabels: { motion: { delay: 40 } },\n      },\n    },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe cascade is renderer default, chart, mark, axis, specific guide, then the\nactive focus-state transition. Same-type transitions inherit omitted fields.\nSet `motion: false` at any definition scope to suppress inherited motion for\nthat scope. A more specific child can re-enable motion with its own definition.\nAn authored `delay` replaces automatic entrance staggering for that target.\nSpring updates begin immediately even when a definition returns a delay, so a\nretarget cannot freeze incoming momentum. Spring enter and exit delays, and\ntween delays in every phase, are honored.\n\nAll built-in marks accept `ChartMarkMotionOptions<TDatum>`. Nested polar marks\nalso accept `motion`; their timing is merged below the parent `polar` mark.\n\n| Scope                          | Motion input                                         |\n| ------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| Renderer fallback              | `motion({ transition })`                             |\n| Whole chart                    | `defineChart({ motion })`                            |\n| Any built-in mark              | The mark's `motion` option                           |\n| Axis, including its grid lines | `x.axis.motion` or `y.axis.motion`                   |\n| Tick rules                     | `axis.ticks.motion`                                  |\n| Tick labels                    | `axis.tickLabels.motion`                             |\n| Axis label                     | `axis.label.motion` when `label` is an object        |\n| Crosshair or focus guide       | The guide mark's `motion` option                     |\n| HTML tooltip                   | Inherits chart motion; `tooltip.motion` overrides it |\n\nGrid lines use their axis policy because they are part of that axis's guide\nsystem. `tooltip.motion: false` keeps the tooltip immediate even when chart\ngeometry animates. Legends and application-owned controls are not marks and do\nnot currently participate in the motion cascade.\n\n## Spreading timing utilities\n\nUse the isolated timing entry when the definition only needs motion policy:\n\n```ts\nimport { stagger } from '@tanstack/charts/motion/definition'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  motion: {\n    path: 'morph',\n    ...stagger({ each: 35, by: 'series', roles: ['arc', 'bar'] }),\n  },\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n`stagger()` returns one context-aware `delay` field for direct object spread.\nIt uses `datumIndex` by default, can use `seriesIndex`, defaults to the `enter`\nphase, and can filter by phase and semantic role. `offset` delays the first\ntarget. Normal object-spread order controls precedence, so an explicit `delay`\nwritten after `...stagger()` replaces it.\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartMotionStaggerOptions {\n  each: number\n  offset?: number\n  by?: 'datum' | 'series'\n  phase?: ChartMotionPhase | readonly ChartMotionPhase[]\n  roles?: ChartMotionRole | readonly ChartMotionRole[]\n}\n```\n\n`stagger()` is also exported from `@tanstack/charts/motion`. The dedicated\n`/motion/definition` entry excludes the SVG renderer and spring solver.\n\n## Timing types\n\n```ts\ntype ChartMotionPhase = 'enter' | 'update' | 'exit'\n\ninterface ChartMotionTweenTransition {\n  type: 'tween'\n  duration?: number\n  easing?:\n    | 'linear'\n    | 'ease'\n    | 'ease-in'\n    | 'ease-out'\n    | 'ease-in-out'\n    | ((progress: number) => number)\n}\n\ninterface ChartMotionSpringTransition extends ChartSpringOptions {\n  type: 'spring'\n}\n\ntype ChartMotionTransition =\n  ChartMotionTweenTransition | ChartMotionSpringTransition\n\ninterface ChartRollingPathMotion {\n  update: 'rolling'\n  x: 'shift'\n  y?: 'fixed' | 'reproject'\n  fallback?: 'snap' | 'morph'\n}\n\ntype ChartMotionPath = 'morph' | ChartRollingPathMotion\n\ninterface ChartMotionTiming<TDatum = unknown> {\n  delay?: number | ((context: ChartMotionContext<TDatum>) => number | undefined)\n  transition?: ChartMotionTransition\n  path?: ChartMotionPath\n}\n\ntype ChartMotionDefinition<TDatum = unknown> =\n  | false\n  | ChartMotionTiming<TDatum>\n  | ((\n      context: ChartMotionContext<TDatum>,\n    ) => false | ChartMotionTiming<TDatum> | undefined)\n\ninterface ChartMarkMotionOptions<TDatum = unknown> {\n  motion?: ChartMotionDefinition<TDatum>\n}\n```\n\n`ChartMotionContext` provides `phase`, semantic `role`, stable `key`, optional\n`markId`, `seriesKey`, `seriesIndex`, `datumIndex`, `datumCount`, optional typed\n`datum` and `point`, and optional `axis`. `ChartMotionRole` covers marks, axes,\ngrid lines, ticks, tick labels, and axis labels.\n\nFocus styles use `ChartMarkStateTransition`, which is a\n`ChartMotionTransition` plus optional `respectReducedMotion`.\n\n`path: 'morph'` is the ordinary command-by-command path interpolation. A\nrolling update is configured only with the `ChartRollingPathMotion` object.\n\n## Rolling paths\n\nPath morphing matches SVG path commands. That is useful when values change in\nplace, but a rolling time series should keep each retained sample intact and\nmove the trace left:\n\n```ts\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  motion: {\n    path: {\n      update: 'rolling',\n      x: 'shift',\n      y: 'reproject',\n      fallback: 'snap',\n    },\n    transition: { type: 'tween', duration: 800, easing: 'linear' },\n  },\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'time', y: 'value', key: 'id' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc().domain([visibleStart, visibleEnd]) },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 100]) },\n  },\n\n  clip: true,\n})\n```\n\nInclude enough keyed samples before `visibleStart` to cover the largest\nexpected update batch. A valid rolling update installs the completed path\nbeyond the right clip, applies one affine transform that reproduces the prior\nframe, and animates that transform to identity. Retained samples therefore move\nas one piece while new samples enter from the right.\n\nThe renderer validates the update before animating it:\n\n- retained keys must be the exact old suffix and new prefix;\n- the keyed primitive must remain a line or remain an area;\n- added and removed sample counts must balance;\n- retained semantic x, y, interval, and group values must be unchanged;\n- every retained x coordinate must share one translation;\n- the plot bounds must be stable and the path must be inside a clip;\n- neither the previous nor target path may have a nonzero transient x or y\n  viewport translation;\n- the target geometry must cover both clip edges throughout the shift;\n- custom path strings and neighbor-sensitive curves do not use the affine\n  path.\n\nOmitting `y` is equivalent to `y: 'fixed'` and requires unchanged screen y\ncoordinates. `y: 'reproject'` also\naccepts a changed continuous y-domain when one affine y transform maps the new\nprojection back to the previous frame. This preserves the path geometry while\nthe line shifts and the y-axis updates. Keep an area's baseline semantically\nstable, such as `y1: 0`, so both edges can be reprojected.\n\n`fallback` defaults to `'snap'`. An update that does not satisfy the rolling\ncontract is installed without an unrelated path morph. Use\n`fallback: 'morph'` only when command interpolation is the intended failure\nbehavior.\n\nA valid update that interrupts another rolling update composes the transform\ncurrently painted on the path with the next shift, then continues toward the\nnew target. It does not reset to either completed scene. Presentation points,\nfocus marks, and an active tooltip follow the same composed geometry.\n\nAuthored point dots and default focus circles use the path's rolling timing.\nEntering points begin under the composed transform, exiting points travel out\nthrough the clip, and snap fallback removes stale exiting presentation points\nin the same commit as the path. A focus layer can update immediately during\ndata motion; geometry or style from an inline mark state waits until the active\ndata transition settles so it cannot cancel the rolling transform. Back-to-back\nupdates retain the latest requested state and apply it when the feed becomes\nidle; a feed that never becomes idle keeps state geometry deferred while focus\nlayers and tooltips continue to follow presentation points.\n\nRolling motion rejects a nonzero `viewport.translate` on either axis because\nthe viewport and path would otherwise own competing transient transforms.\nCommit the viewport domain and reset its translation before the rolling data\nupdate, or expect the configured snap-or-morph fallback.\n\nUse fixed plot margins so changing tick-label widths cannot change the affine\nframe. Use linear segments or a curve whose visible control points do not\nchange when a sample is appended.\n\n`motion()` renders SVG clip paths and gradients itself, so `clip: true` applies\nto the animated marks without a separate resource renderer.\n\n## Standalone spring\n\n```ts\nimport { createChartSpring } from '@tanstack/charts/spring'\n\nconst spring = createChartSpring({ stiffness: 170, damping: 18, mass: 1 })\nconst sample = spring.sample(16, { from: 0, to: 100, velocity: 0 })\n```\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartSpringOptions {\n  stiffness?: number\n  damping?: number\n  mass?: number\n  restSpeed?: number\n  restDelta?: number\n}\n\ninterface ChartSpringState {\n  from: number\n  to: number\n  velocity?: number\n}\n\ninterface ChartSpringSample {\n  value: number\n  velocity: number\n  done: boolean\n}\n\ninterface ChartSpring {\n  readonly options: Readonly<Required<ChartSpringOptions>>\n  sample(elapsedMs: number, state?: ChartSpringState): ChartSpringSample\n}\n```\n\n`createChartSpring` returns an analytic, frame-rate-independent damped harmonic\noscillator. Values and velocities use caller units per second. Seed a new\n`ChartSpringState` from the prior sample to preserve momentum across targets.\n\n## Compatibility\n\n- Compatible numeric attributes and path command skeletons interpolate.\n- Incompatible element types or path topology use keyed enter/exit opacity.\n- Stable keys preserve DOM identity, velocity, and presentation points.\n- Crosshair rules, bands, labels, and markers follow the same keyed interruption\n  behavior as focused marks.\n- SVG interaction follows animated presentation geometry for keyed built-in and\n  custom marks.\n- Static SVG and Canvas ignore definition motion and paint final geometry.\n- The optional renderer is browser SVG only; it is not a Web Animations, View\n  Transitions, Canvas, or native adapter.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Motion\ndescription: Optional SVG tween and spring motion, definition-local timing, interruption behavior, reduced motion, and the standalone spring sampler.\n---\n\nMotion is an optional renderer. Core definitions, scene compilation, static SVG,\nand Canvas do not import a clock or physics solver.\n\n## `motion`\n\n```ts\nimport { motion } from '@tanstack/charts/motion'\n\nconst renderer = motion({\n  transition: { type: 'spring', stiffness: 170, damping: 18, mass: 1 },\n})\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction motion(options?: ChartMotionOptions): UniversalChartRenderer\n\nfunction motion<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n>(options?: ChartMotionOptions): ChartRenderer<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n\ninterface ChartMotionOptions {\n  initial?: boolean | 'always'\n  transition?: ChartMotionTransition\n  respectReducedMotion?: boolean\n  resize?: boolean\n}\n```\n\nUse `motion(options)` beside a typed definition and let the host infer its\ndatum and axis values. The explicit generic overload remains available when a\nlow-level caller must type the renderer before it has a definition.\n\n| Option                 | Default                                       | Meaning                                                       |\n| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `initial`              | `true`                                        | Animate first client paint; `always` also replays adopted SVG |\n| `transition`           | 1,100 ms tween with the default entrance ease | Renderer-wide fallback                                        |\n| `respectReducedMotion` | `true`                                        | Snap when `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` matches            |\n| `resize`               | `false`                                       | Animate updates caused only by a chart size change            |\n\nServer-rendered SVG is adopted without replaying entrance motion by default.\nSet `initial: 'always'` when a hydrated chart should replay the same entrance\nas a client-only mount. Keyed updates start from painted geometry. An\ninterrupted spring carries its sampled value and velocity into the new target.\nA spring has no duration; it finishes when both `restSpeed` and `restDelta` are\nsatisfied, with a 10-second safety limit.\n\nInitial choreography follows geometry: Cartesian bars and paths grow from\ntheir semantic baseline, radial lines and areas grow from the polar center,\nand arcs sweep through their authored angle. Keyed removals stay painted\nthrough their exit transition.\n\nData-less `crosshair` marks use the same keyed focus-motion path. Rapid pointer\nor keyboard retargeting preserves the guide elements and incoming spring\nvelocity; labels remain aligned to their moving rules.\n\nInline mark `states` wait while entrance or data motion is active. The latest\nstate is applied when that motion settles. Applying another geometry\ntransition to the same marks would interrupt the running data transition.\nThis includes radius changes such as growing a focused dot. A continuously\nupdating chart can keep these states deferred.\n\nUse a separate `whenFocused` mark for a focus indicator that must appear\nduring entrance motion. The focus layer has its own visibility and does not\nreplace the animated mark's geometry:\n\n```ts\nimport { dot, lineY, whenFocused } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst marks = [\n  lineY(rows, { x: 'x', y: 'y' }),\n  dot(rows, { x: 'x', y: 'y', r: 2 }),\n  whenFocused(dot(rows, { id: 'focus-dots', x: 'x', y: 'y', r: 6 }), {\n    match: 'x',\n  }),\n]\n```\n\nThe built-in HTML tooltip also consumes this renderer's transition. Entry,\nmovement, retargeting, and exit therefore use the same spring without copying\nthe transition into the chart definition. A static renderer keeps the tooltip\nimmediate and does not import the motion runtime.\n\nUse the renderer-neutral host in vanilla applications:\n\n```ts\nimport { mountChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/renderer'\nimport { motion } from '@tanstack/charts/motion'\n\nconst host = mountChartRenderer(container, {\n  definition,\n  renderer: motion(),\n  width: 640,\n  height: 360,\n  ariaLabel: 'Monthly revenue',\n})\n```\n\nReact and Octane applications use their `/core` component entry and pass the\nsame renderer. Other adapters start with their default SVG renderer, and a\ndefinition may still select Canvas for individual marks.\n\n## Definition-local motion\n\n`motion` on a definition, mark, axis, tick collection, tick-label collection,\nor axis label is inert policy. The optional renderer consumes it. Definitions\nremain valid for static SVG and Canvas, which paint the final state.\n\nThis stays true in a mixed chart. A mark that selects `canvasChartRenderer`\nkeeps its authored motion option, but the Canvas layer paints the final scene\ninstead of running the tween or spring policy. The optional `motion()` renderer\nstill animates the default-renderer layers it owns.\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  motion: {\n    transition: { type: 'spring', stiffness: 170, damping: 18 },\n  },\n  marks: [\n    lineY(rows, {\n      id: 'forecast',\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'forecast',\n      key: 'id',\n      motion: { transition: { type: 'spring', mass: 1.25 } },\n    }),\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'actual',\n      key: 'id',\n      motion(context) {\n        return {\n          delay: context.phase === 'enter' ? context.datumIndex * 35 : 0,\n        }\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleBand,\n      axis: {\n        ticks: { motion: { transition: { type: 'tween', duration: 180 } } },\n        tickLabels: { motion: { delay: 40 } },\n      },\n    },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe cascade is renderer default, chart, mark, axis, specific guide, then the\nactive focus-state transition. Same-type transitions inherit omitted fields.\nSet `motion: false` at any definition scope to suppress inherited motion for\nthat scope. A more specific child can re-enable motion with its own definition.\nAn authored `delay` replaces automatic entrance staggering for that target.\nSpring updates begin immediately even when a definition returns a delay, so a\nretarget cannot freeze incoming momentum. Spring enter and exit delays, and\ntween delays in every phase, are honored.\n\nAll built-in marks accept `ChartMarkMotionOptions<TDatum>`. Nested polar marks\nalso accept `motion`; their timing is merged below the parent `polar` mark.\n\n| Scope                          | Motion input                                         |\n| ------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| Renderer fallback              | `motion({ transition })`                             |\n| Whole chart                    | `defineChart({ motion })`                            |\n| Any built-in mark              | The mark's `motion` option                           |\n| Axis, including its grid lines | `x.axis.motion` or `y.axis.motion`                   |\n| Tick rules                     | `axis.ticks.motion`                                  |\n| Tick labels                    | `axis.tickLabels.motion`                             |\n| Axis label                     | `axis.label.motion` when `label` is an object        |\n| Crosshair or focus guide       | The guide mark's `motion` option                     |\n| HTML tooltip                   | Inherits chart motion; `tooltip.motion` overrides it |\n\nGrid lines use their axis policy because they are part of that axis's guide\nsystem. `tooltip.motion: false` keeps the tooltip immediate even when chart\ngeometry animates. Legends and application-owned controls are not marks and do\nnot currently participate in the motion cascade.\n\n## Spreading timing utilities\n\nUse the isolated timing entry when the definition only needs motion policy:\n\n```ts\nimport { stagger } from '@tanstack/charts/motion/definition'\n\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  motion: {\n    path: 'morph',\n    ...stagger({ each: 35, by: 'series', roles: ['arc', 'bar'] }),\n  },\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n`stagger()` returns one context-aware `delay` field for direct object spread.\nIt uses `datumIndex` by default, can use `seriesIndex`, defaults to the `enter`\nphase, and can filter by phase and semantic role. `offset` delays the first\ntarget. Normal object-spread order controls precedence, so an explicit `delay`\nwritten after `...stagger()` replaces it.\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartMotionStaggerOptions {\n  each: number\n  offset?: number\n  by?: 'datum' | 'series'\n  phase?: ChartMotionPhase | readonly ChartMotionPhase[]\n  roles?: ChartMotionRole | readonly ChartMotionRole[]\n}\n```\n\n`stagger()` is also exported from `@tanstack/charts/motion`. The dedicated\n`/motion/definition` entry excludes the SVG renderer and spring solver.\n\n## Timing types\n\n```ts\ntype ChartMotionPhase = 'enter' | 'update' | 'exit'\n\ninterface ChartMotionTweenTransition {\n  type: 'tween'\n  duration?: number\n  easing?:\n    | 'linear'\n    | 'ease'\n    | 'ease-in'\n    | 'ease-out'\n    | 'ease-in-out'\n    | ((progress: number) => number)\n}\n\ninterface ChartMotionSpringTransition extends ChartSpringOptions {\n  type: 'spring'\n}\n\ntype ChartMotionTransition =\n  ChartMotionTweenTransition | ChartMotionSpringTransition\n\ninterface ChartRollingPathMotion {\n  update: 'rolling'\n  x: 'shift'\n  y?: 'fixed' | 'reproject'\n  fallback?: 'snap' | 'morph'\n}\n\ntype ChartMotionPath = 'morph' | ChartRollingPathMotion\n\ninterface ChartMotionTiming<TDatum = unknown> {\n  delay?: number | ((context: ChartMotionContext<TDatum>) => number | undefined)\n  transition?: ChartMotionTransition\n  path?: ChartMotionPath\n}\n\ntype ChartMotionDefinition<TDatum = unknown> =\n  | false\n  | ChartMotionTiming<TDatum>\n  | ((\n      context: ChartMotionContext<TDatum>,\n    ) => false | ChartMotionTiming<TDatum> | undefined)\n\ninterface ChartMarkMotionOptions<TDatum = unknown> {\n  motion?: ChartMotionDefinition<TDatum>\n}\n```\n\n`ChartMotionContext` provides `phase`, semantic `role`, stable `key`, optional\n`markId`, `seriesKey`, `seriesIndex`, `datumIndex`, `datumCount`, optional typed\n`datum` and `point`, and optional `axis`. `ChartMotionRole` covers marks, axes,\ngrid lines, ticks, tick labels, and axis labels.\n\nFocus styles use `ChartMarkStateTransition`, which is a\n`ChartMotionTransition` plus optional `respectReducedMotion`.\n\n`path: 'morph'` is the ordinary command-by-command path interpolation. A\nrolling update is configured only with the `ChartRollingPathMotion` object.\n\n## Rolling paths\n\nPath morphing matches SVG path commands. That is useful when values change in\nplace, but a rolling time series should keep each retained sample intact and\nmove the trace left:\n\n```ts\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  motion: {\n    path: {\n      update: 'rolling',\n      x: 'shift',\n      y: 'reproject',\n      fallback: 'snap',\n    },\n    transition: { type: 'tween', duration: 800, easing: 'linear' },\n  },\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'time', y: 'value', key: 'id' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleUtc().domain([visibleStart, visibleEnd]) },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 100]) },\n  },\n\n  clip: true,\n})\n```\n\nInclude enough keyed samples before `visibleStart` to cover the largest\nexpected update batch. A valid rolling update installs the completed path\nbeyond the right clip, applies one affine transform that reproduces the prior\nframe, and animates that transform to identity. Retained samples therefore move\nas one piece while new samples enter from the right.\n\nThe renderer validates the update before animating it:\n\n- retained keys must be the exact old suffix and new prefix;\n- the keyed primitive must remain a line or remain an area;\n- added and removed sample counts must balance;\n- retained semantic x, y, interval, and group values must be unchanged;\n- every retained x coordinate must share one translation;\n- the plot bounds must be stable and the path must be inside a clip;\n- neither the previous nor target path may have a nonzero transient x or y\n  viewport translation;\n- the target geometry must cover both clip edges throughout the shift;\n- custom path strings and neighbor-sensitive curves do not use the affine\n  path.\n\nOmitting `y` is equivalent to `y: 'fixed'` and requires unchanged screen y\ncoordinates. `y: 'reproject'` also\naccepts a changed continuous y-domain when one affine y transform maps the new\nprojection back to the previous frame. This preserves the path geometry while\nthe line shifts and the y-axis updates. Keep an area's baseline semantically\nstable, such as `y1: 0`, so both edges can be reprojected.\n\n`fallback` defaults to `'snap'`. An update that does not satisfy the rolling\ncontract is installed without an unrelated path morph. Use\n`fallback: 'morph'` only when command interpolation is the intended failure\nbehavior.\n\nA valid update that interrupts another rolling update composes the transform\ncurrently painted on the path with the next shift, then continues toward the\nnew target. It does not reset to either completed scene. Presentation points,\nfocus marks, and an active tooltip follow the same composed geometry.\n\nAuthored point dots and default focus circles use the path's rolling timing.\nEntering points begin under the composed transform, exiting points travel out\nthrough the clip, and snap fallback removes stale exiting presentation points\nin the same commit as the path. A focus layer can update immediately during\ndata motion; geometry or style from an inline mark state waits until the active\ndata transition settles so it cannot cancel the rolling transform. Back-to-back\nupdates retain the latest requested state and apply it when the feed becomes\nidle; a feed that never becomes idle keeps state geometry deferred while focus\nlayers and tooltips continue to follow presentation points.\n\nRolling motion rejects a nonzero `viewport.translate` on either axis because\nthe viewport and path would otherwise own competing transient transforms.\nCommit the viewport domain and reset its translation before the rolling data\nupdate, or expect the configured snap-or-morph fallback.\n\nUse fixed plot margins so changing tick-label widths cannot change the affine\nframe. Use linear segments or a curve whose visible control points do not\nchange when a sample is appended.\n\n`motion()` renders SVG clip paths and gradients itself, so `clip: true` applies\nto the animated marks without a separate resource renderer.\n\n## Standalone spring\n\n```ts\nimport { createChartSpring } from '@tanstack/charts/spring'\n\nconst spring = createChartSpring({ stiffness: 170, damping: 18, mass: 1 })\nconst sample = spring.sample(16, { from: 0, to: 100, velocity: 0 })\n```\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartSpringOptions {\n  stiffness?: number\n  damping?: number\n  mass?: number\n  restSpeed?: number\n  restDelta?: number\n}\n\ninterface ChartSpringState {\n  from: number\n  to: number\n  velocity?: number\n}\n\ninterface ChartSpringSample {\n  value: number\n  velocity: number\n  done: boolean\n}\n\ninterface ChartSpring {\n  readonly options: Readonly<Required<ChartSpringOptions>>\n  sample(elapsedMs: number, state?: ChartSpringState): ChartSpringSample\n}\n```\n\n`createChartSpring` returns an analytic, frame-rate-independent damped harmonic\noscillator. Values and velocities use caller units per second. Seed a new\n`ChartSpringState` from the prior sample to preserve momentum across targets.\n\n## Compatibility\n\n- Compatible numeric attributes and path command skeletons interpolate.\n- Incompatible element types or path topology use keyed enter/exit opacity.\n- Stable keys preserve DOM identity, velocity, and presentation points.\n- Crosshair rules, bands, labels, and markers follow the same keyed interruption\n  behavior as focused marks.\n- SVG interaction follows animated presentation geometry for keyed built-in and\n  custom marks.\n- Static SVG and Canvas ignore definition motion and paint final geometry.\n- The optional renderer is browser SVG only; it is not a Web Animations, View\n  Transitions, Canvas, or native adapter.\n"}
